('CS2000','1','Ethics in Data Security and AI','4','This course explores the ethical challenges and considerations in the fields of Data Security and Artificial Intelligence (AI). Students will examine the moral implications of data collection, storage, and usage, with a particular focus on privacy, consent, and transparency. The course will delve into the ethical dilemmas posed by AI systems, including bias, fairness, accountability, and the societal impact of generative AI and autonomous technologies. Through case studies, discussions, debates ','Undergraduate'),
('CS2510','1','Computer Programming I','4','An introduction to problem solving through programming. Topics include program design and analysis: algorithmic processes, fundamental techniques of program development, debugging and testing. Programming projects and applications will be written in a structured computer language. Students learn the basic language features of C++/Java to write basic to intermediate level programs. Offered every semester.','Undergraduate'),
('CS2511','1','Computer Programming II','4','Continuation of CS 2510. Discussion of methods, arrays, class and object, inhritance, polymorphism, exceptions, file operations, and GUI (Graphical User Interface) applications. Basic data structures and algorithms like insertion sort, binary search in a sorted array, and recursion may also be discussed if tme allows. Students write intermediate to advanced level programs in C++/Java.','Undergraduate'),
('CS2521','1','Scientific Programming in Python','4','A fast paced introduction to program design and analysis: algorithmic processes, basic programming techniques, program specification & structure, program development, debugging and testing. Emphasis on scientific applications. Discussion of storage classes, files and string manipulation. Basic data structures and algorithms, data abstractions and object-oriented programming. Students learn the language features of Python with an eye toward scientific and data analysis applications.','Undergraduate'),
('CS2550','1','Building AI tools','4','This course equips students with the skills and knowledge necessary to design, implement, and optimize AI-enhanced data management systems. The course is research focused where students from different backgrounds would work together to on a project. The course would be based on leveraging recent advancements in machine learning and artificial intelligence including large language models. The course would be project based and project ideas and topics could come from the local community.','Undergraduate'),
('CS2700','1','Introduction to Security Fundamentals','4','This course provides an essential introduction to the principles and practices of computer security, equipping students with the knowledge and skills needed to protect information systems. The course covers foundational topics in cybersecurity, including network security, cryptography, malware analysis, access control, and incident response. Students will explore the types of cyber threats faced by organizations, from individual hackers to organized cybercriminals, and learn how to develop effec','Undergraduate'),
('CS3550','1','Applied Database','4','Study of database fundamentals including relational, document and graph databases. The course introduces ERD Design, Relational algebra, interacting with database with SQL, Advanced SQL: Trigger, stored procedures. Functional dependencies and Normalization.','Undergraduate'),
('CS3611','1','Advanced C#','4','This course provides an in-depth study of the skills and knowledge of C# programming. It is intended to reinforce and build upon the introductory programming course by introducing more advanced features of C# programming language and environment. Some of the advanced topics include SQL, database programming, reporting scheme, programming for the web and web forms, object-oriented programming, accessing data with ADO.NET. It also introduces basic technologies in ASP .NET.','Undergraduate'),
('CS3620','1','Computer Architecture I','4','Basic computer architecture. Switching theory, data representation, arithmetic operations, assembly language coding, Boolean logic, combinational circuits, logical design, instruction execution, sequential machines.','Undergraduate'),
('CS3630','1','Computer Architecture II','4','Integrated circuits, pipelining, Cache Memory, DRAM, RAID, Memory Hierarchy, I/O Device Interfacing, DMA, CISC processing, RISC processing, Multi processing, Vector processing, Parallel processing.','Undergraduate'),
('CS3810','1','Data Structures and Algorithms','4','Introduces abstract data structures and their implementations, including lists, stacks, queues, trees, hash tables, heaps and linked structures. Analyze and design sorting and searching algorithms. Learn computational evaluation on performance of algorithms. Advanced level programming. Assignments conforming to Object-Oriented methodology covered in CS2511.','Undergraduate'),
('CS3911','1','C++ and Object-Oriented Programming','4','An object oriented approach to C++ with an emphasis on how to self-learn a new programming language. Will reiterate major concepts such as encapsulation, abstraction, classes, and objects as well as learn about pointers and dynamic memory. Will introduce data structures high level design patterns in C++ and include other language features such as programming with STL and templates.','Undergraduate'),
('CS4100','1','Technical Communications','4','Emphasis on technical writing and public speaking skills. Application of report-writing fundamentals to technical correspondence, technical reports, user manuals. Detailed analysis of the problem of conveying technical information to technical and non-technical audiences in written, as well as oral form. Term project involving written paper on a technical subject and an oral presentation of the subject in class.','Undergraduate'),
('CS4200','1','Mobile Programming through Android','4','This course introduces students to creating mobile applications through the Android programming environment. Students will build upon their programming experience in Java to learn the fundamentals of event driven multitier programming, including important programming patterns that best fit the unique needs of mobile devices. Furthermore, students will learn how to create interactive GUIs that leverage mobile capabilities, including data from embedded devices such as cameras and sensors such as G','Undergraduate'),
('CS4310','1','Design and Analysis of Algorithms','4','This course offers a comprehensive and rigorous exploration of algorithm design and analysis, grounded in fundamental data structures and essential mathematical principles. Core topics include efficient sorting and searching algorithms, algorithmic paradigms such as divide-and-conquer, dynamic programming, greedy methods, and amortized analysis. Students will engage with advanced techniques including selection algorithms and linear programming. The course places particular emphasis on graph algo','Undergraduate'),
('CS4400','1','Artificial Intelligence','4','Introduction to the programming language LISP . Additional topics include: search as problem-solving paradigm; depth first, breadth first, best first, min/max and alpha beta; state space representation, OR graphs, AND/OR graphs, means end analysis and problem reduction; propositional and predicate logic, skolemization, resolution 181 and theorem proving, semantic nets, frames, neural nets and genetic algorithms. Students write programs in LISP and C++/Java.','Undergraduate'),
('CS4420','1','Machiine Learning','4','This course covers fundamental machine learning concepts for intelligent systems that autonomously learn to perform a task and improve with experience, including problem formulations (e.g., selecting input features and outputs) and learning frameworks (e.g., supervised, unsupervised, semi-supervised), standard models, methods, computational tools, algorithms and modern techniques, as well as methodologies to evaluate learning ability and to automatically select optimal models with hands-on learn','Undergraduate'),
('CS4501','1','Software Engineering','4','Introduces principles and disciplines of software engineering. Semester-long projects allow students to practice on a complete process of the software development. A software product will be implemented and tested at the end. The Object-oriented software engineering with the UML modeling approach is applied on projects. Learn system decomposition and design patterns. The project management aspect and risk controls are also introduced and practiced in the projects. Individual and team works.','Undergraduate'),
('CS4550','1','Database Management Systems','4','Basic concepts: data, information systems, data independence and need for DBMS facilities. The relational model: schema, subschema, relational algebra, relational calculus, SQL, ODBC, JDBC and SQLJ. Database design: entityrelationship model and normalization. Performance considerations, integrity, security and transaction processing. DBMS examples: Access, DB2 and Oracle.','Undergraduate'),
('CS4600','1','Topics in Computer Science','4','Current topics in computer science which are of interest to the computer science faculty will be chosen for discussion. Topics may change each semester.','Undergraduate'),
('CS4705','1','Introduction to Computer Security','4','This course covers basic ideas of computer and network security and fundamental techniques for defending computers and networks against adversaries. The course introduces the students to: secure software engineering, operating system security, network security, cryptography, cryptographic protocols, denial of service attacks, firewalls, authentication techniques, web and server security. Students will work on programming projects individually and/or jointly in groups.','Undergraduate'),
('CS4710','1','Applied Cryptography','4','This course develops notions of information security and deals with the tools for guaranteeing secure communications in an adversarial setting. Topics include basic number theory, cryptographic methods, key distribution, and protocols for authentication and confidential communications. The course examines C++/Java security applications that employ cryptography. Students will be assigned several small programs incorporating security.','Undergraduate'),
('CS4720','1','Internet and Web Technologies','4','Introduce the Internet, the World Wide Web, the client/server applications, and various web development technologies. Teach hot techniques currently adopted in web application industry including languages for front-end applications such as XHTML, CSS, DHTML, JavaScript and languages for back-end applications such as JavaServer Page, JavaServer Face, PHP , the .Net platform and Python. The XML and Ajax is also introduced.','Undergraduate'),
('CS5610','1','Operating Systems','4','Memory management: multiprogramming, relocation, paging, virtual memory, dynamic loading and linking. Process management: job scheduling, process scheduling and synchronization, multiprocessing. Device manage182 ment: input/output, channels and control units, buffering, interrupt structure and processing. Information management: logical and physical file systems, file processing. OS examples: Unix/Linux, Windows.','Undergraduate'),
('CS5620','1','Computer Science Practicum','4','This is an internship course which is supervised by a full-time computer science faculty member and can be repeated. Students must have obtained an internship approved by the CIS curriculum committee and credits CANNOT count as CIS upper division elective credits.','Undergraduate'),
('CS5710','1','Computer Networks','4','Introduces principles and disciplines of software engineering. Semester-long projects allow students to practice on a complete process of the software development. A software product will be implemented and tested at the end. The Object-oriented software engineering with the UML modeling approach is applied on projects. Learn system decomposition and design patterns. The project management aspect and risk controls are also introduced and practiced in the projects. Individual and team works.','Undergraduate'),
('CS5730','1','Computer Network Security','4','This course is a continuation of CS4710 and deals with the issues in network security. Topics include Kerberos, public key infrastructures, electronic mail security, IP security, Web security, intrusion detection and response, password management, viruses and worms, firewalls, denial of service attacks and defenses, wireless security, and privacy. Students will write several small programs including a network application incorporating security.','Undergraduate'),
('CS5800','1','Data Warehousing','4','This course introduces the major activities involved in data warehousing application design and implementation. The course starts with an in-depth discussion of the basic concepts and principles of data warehousing, then studies the changes dictated by big data analytics. We discuss the MapReduce framework and its implementation Hadoop and the higher level language HiveQL. We discuss the two popular database architectures, column store databases and inmemoryDBS. We also discuss real-time data wa','Undergraduate'),
('CS5810','1','Data Mining I','4','This course covers the fundamental concepts and techniques useful in discovering knowledge hidden in large data sets. Topics include data preprocessing, frequent pattern matching, classification, clustering and database methods. The course also discusses online analytic processing (OLAP), outlier detection, and important applications. The course assignments will be carried out individually and in groups.','Undergraduate'),
('CS5820','1','Neural Networks & Deep Learning','4','The majority of data in the world is unlabeled and unstructured, for instance images, sound, and text data. Shallow neural networks cannot easily capture relevant structures within this type of data, but deep networks are capable of discovering the hidden structures. Therefore, the demand for deep learning skills is continuing to grow, as AI becomes more pervasive in our societies. In this course, you will learn about some of the inspiring and motivating applications of deep learning. You will l','Undergraduate'),
('CS5821','1','Neural Networks & Deep Learning','4','The majority of data in the world is unlabeled and unstructured, for instance images, sound, and text data. Shallow neural networks cannot easily capture relevant structures within this type of data, but deep networks are capable of discovering the hidden structures. Therefore, the demand for deep learning skills is continuing to grow, as AI becomes more pervasive in our societies. In this course, you will learn about some of the inspiring and motivating applications of deep learning. You will l','Undergraduate'),
('CS5910','1','System Design & Implementation','4','System feasibility studies, meeting with users, project definition, environmental analysis. Data collection, design phase, system restart and recovery. Database considerations, programming, system completion, systems parallel, benchmarks. User training and manual. Conversion planning. Students, working in groups, design and implement a significant software project.','Undergraduate'),
('ED3330','8','Children’s Literature','4','An introduction to the study of literature for children in the pre‑school and elementary grades. The course includes, selection and evaluation of books for children; language development through children’s literature; use of literature to promote the social, emotional and creative development of children; integrating children’s literature into the content areas; planning story activities. Opportunities to work with children ages 3‑7 where feasible.','Undergraduate'),
('ED3700','8','Child Development','4','Provides students with background information concerning the intellectual, physiological, social, emotional, and creative development of children from birth through the elementary school years. Emphasis is given to the contributions of Piaget, Gardner and other developmental theorists. Practical relevance of child development information is stressed throughout this course for students who plan to work with children. Students will participate in a practicum in a field- based classroom to gain exp','Undergraduate'),
('ED3742','8','Middle Childhood and Adolescence','4','Examines human development from six years through adolescence. Research and theories concerning physical, cognitive, personality and social development will be reviewed. Family, peer groups, schools and media will be discussed. The impact of socioeconomic background, culture, race and gender will be discussed throughout. The use of theories and research in applied settings will be discussed.','Undergraduate'),
('ED3800','8','Human Development and Learning','4','This course focuses on the development of learners from birth through adolescence, their phases of learning, their intellectual, social, and emotional growth, character development, individual differences, interests, attention, potential challenges, and motivation. Candidates will examine how these aspects of human development relate and influence the teaching and learning process. 15 hours of field experience required.','Undergraduate'),
('ED3820','8','Foundations of Special Education','4','This course offers an introductory overview of the field of special education across the lifespan, which includes the investigation of the development, nature, causes and characteristics of young children and adolescents with special needs. Consistent with the knowledge base of the field, the course is based on an interdisciplinary perspective. We will review an extensive body of information that relates to five broad topics: history of the field, basic notions, current trends, and exceptionalit','Undergraduate'),
('ED3900','8','Foundations of Education','4','This course will offer an introduction to the world of teaching, as it examines the basic theories of learning and teaching across educational environments for the birth-12 learner. Specifically, the course encourages deeper understanding of the educator and student in relation to others; it engages in an exploration of the social/historical/political contexts that affect education and teaching; the significant characteristics that diverse student populations bring to the classroom; and the rela','Undergraduate'),
('ED4050','8','Innovative Instructional Design and Assessment','4','This course provides background information for developing curriculum. Emphasis is given to the preparation of lesson plans and unit plans using backwards design. Students will obtain skills in developing objectives, designing instruction and assessment based on Common Core standards. Throughout the course, students will demonstrate competence by creating lesson plans and a unit plan.','Undergraduate'),
('ED4050H2020','8','Innovative Instructional Design and Assessment','4','This course provides background information for developing curriculum. Emphasis is given to the preparation of lesson plans and unit plans using backwards design. Students will obtain skills in developing objectives, designing instruction and assessment based on Common Core standards. Throughout the course, students will demonstrate competence by creating lesson plans and a unit plan.','Undergraduate'),
('ED4082','8','Methods and Materials of Teaching Mathematics','4','This course is a study of the strategies, techniques, materials, and technology used to teach mathematics to middle childhood and high school students. Candidates will become acquainted with New York State learning standards involved in teaching mathematics at the secondary school level, develop an awareness of the professional environment, and of resources and information available for teachers. There will be a strong focus on unit and lesson planning, assessment methods, interdisciplinary conn','Undergraduate'),
('ED4083','8','Methods and Materials of Teaching Spanish','4','This course is a study of the strategies, techniques, materials, and technology used to teach Spanish to middle childhood and high school students. Candidates will become acquainted with New York State learning standards involved in teaching Spanish at the secondary school level, develop an awareness of the professional environment, and resources and information available for teachers. There will be a strong focus on unit and lesson planning, assessment methods, interdisciplinary connections and','Undergraduate'),
('ED4085','8','Methods and Materials of Teaching Science','4','This course is a study of the strategies, techniques, materials, and technology used to teach science to middle childhood and high school students. Candidates will become acquainted with New York State learning standards involved in teaching science at the secondary school level, develop an awareness of the professional environment, and resources and information available for teachers. There will be a strong focus on unit and lesson planning, assessment methods, interdisciplinary connections and','Undergraduate'),
('ED4086','8','Methods & Materials of Teaching Social Studies','4','This course is a study of the strategies, techniques, materials, and technology used to teach social studies to middle childhood and high school students. Candidates will become acquainted with New York State learning standards involved in teaching social studies at the secondary school level, develop an awareness of the profes sional environment, and resources and information available for teachers. There will be a strong focus on unit and lesson planning, assessment methods, interdisciplinary ','Undergraduate'),
('ED4121','8','Methods and Materials for Teaching Elementary Science','4','This course is designed to develop pre-service teachers’ skills and abilities in teaching science to preschool and elementary school learners through inquiry and investigations. Emphasis is placed on how children learn science, lesson planning, assessment of students’ scientific reasoning, safety practices, issues of diversity, and application of technology in relation to New York State and National Science Standards.','Undergraduate'),
('ED4122','8','Methods and Materials for Teaching Elementary Math','4','This course is designed to introduce principles, methods, and materials for teaching mathematics to diverse learners in an early childhood and elementary classrooms. Emphasis is placed on how children learn mathematics, teaching through problem solving and children’s literature, using different modes of representation including technology to develop relational understanding and procedural fluency, principles of effective mathematics teaching, and Standards for Mathematical Practice. Participants','Undergraduate'),
('ED4200','8','Literacy for All Students','4','This course will examine theory, research, and practice of literacy learning and teaching. Special attention is given to the theoretical reading models, developmental stages of literacy, literacy skills and strategies, literacy assessment, planning for English Language Learners, and integration of learning and teaching standards. This course will include practical applications of methods and materials used in class. A field component is required.','Undergraduate'),
('ED4220','8','Language Arts in the Context of Childhood Education','4','This course explores reading and writing in discipline-specific areas including English Language Arts, Science, Social Studies, and Mathematics for students in the upper elementary grades (3-6). The course places a strong emphasis on disciplinary and interdisciplinary uses and purposes for oral and written texts and includes research-based models for integrating reading and writing across the curriculum. To support the goal of student comprehension, the course emphasizes: (1) various forms of as','Undergraduate'),
('ED4221','8','Teaching Literacy in Multilingual Settings','4','In this course, participants critically examine the underlying theories and research-based literacy teaching and assessment practices for multilingual learners who are developing literacy in more than one language. Participants will design literacy instruction across the curriculum for children in their first language and English as a new language for students from birth through grade six guided by the research-based building blocks of literacy commonly referred to as the Science of Reading. Thr','Undergraduate'),
('ED4225','8','Literacy for Diverse Learners','4','This course is designed to help teachers develop an understanding of the theory, research, and practice of teaching reading and writing in PreK-12th grade classrooms with an added emphasis on the intersection with special and bilingual education. The main goals of the course are: to gain an understanding of how students develop as listeners, speakers, readers, and writers-cognitively, socially, linguistically, culturally; become familiar with a variety of instructional practices that support you','Undergraduate'),
('ED4230','8','Reading Across the Curriculum','4','This course focuses on current approaches to literacy instruction across the curriculum. It is designed for secondary teacher candidates, and addresses specific instructional strategies appropriate for the adolescent population. Emphasis will be placed on theoretical models of reading, instructional strategies, assessment techniques, strategies specific to multicultural and bilingual learners.','Undergraduate'),
('ED4231','8','Reading Across the Curriculum Practicum','2','The purpose of this course is to provide adolescence and middle childhood education candidates with an opportunity to observe literacy strategies in various discipline settings. The course goal is to familiarize candidates with differentiation of instruction and methodology for diverse / multicultural populations. Candidates will also become familiar with the strategic needs of learners with exceptionalities. Candidates will observe students in a bilingual and/or ESL classroom, and maintain a re','Undergraduate'),
('ED4250','8','Linguistics for Language Educators','4','Linguistics for Language Educators focuses on building knowledge related to the ways in which languages work in teaching and learning. This course will benefit language educators of all kinds including bilingual, world language (i.e. Spanish Education at the elementary or secondary level), English-for-Speakers-of-Other-Languages (ESOL) and any teacher in a mainstream or special education classrooms with students designed as English Learners (ELs). This course covers theories of language acquisit','Undergraduate'),
('ED4255','8','Language Development and Instruction for Diverse Learners','4','This course is designed to prepare pre-service teachers for instruction in reading and writing for Birth through 12th- grade students with a focus on students with exceptionalities. Teacher candidates will gain knowledge of pedagogical theories for reading and writing development and evidence-based instructional strategies for students across disciplines and settings. The development of language and learning will be explored, as well as a balanced and integrated language arts curriculum, includi','Undergraduate'),
('ED4280','8','Methods and Materials for Teaching Mathematics and Sciences to Diverse','4','Students Credits: 4 In this course candidates will learn different methods and materials for teaching and modifying instruction in mathematics, sciences, and technology for diverse students in Birth -12th grade classrooms. It will provide a forum for the discussion of different issues associated with the field of learning disabilities and special education. More specifically students will have the opportunity to examine a wide range of effective age-appropriate instructional technology and accom','Undergraduate'),
('ED4300','8','Assessment, Evaluation, and Evidence-based Decision Making in Special','4','Education Credits: 4 The purpose of ED 4300 is to provide pre-service teachers with skills needed to use assessment data to make diagnostic and instructional decisions for students classified with disabilities from birth through 12th grade. Pre-service teachers will learn how to determine students’ strengths and areas of need by monitoring student progress across disciplines and settings, conducting error analyses, conducting norm and criterion-referenced assessments, and using assessment result','Undergraduate'),
('ED4670','8','Methods and Materials of Teaching English as a Second Language','4','This course will prepare pre-service teachers to make connections between theory and methodology for the teaching of English as a Second Language in bilingual classrooms. The course will emphasize the needs of English learners (ELs) in academic instruction, with a focus on language and literacy integration throughout the curriculum, content-based ESL, and reading and writing strategies for English language learners. The course will provide interactive exploration of research-based ESL classroom ','Undergraduate'),
('ED4810','8','Positive Behavior Supports and Interventions for Students with Disabilities','4','This course is designed to provide students with an understanding of the theoretical foundations of both classroom management and behavior, and focuses on a wide range of proactive and preventative approaches to supporting positive student behavior. Additional course topics include the application of evidence-based approaches to addressing challenging behaviors in Birth through Grade 12 classroom settings, including the collection & analysis of student behavioral data, and the creation function-','Undergraduate'),
('ED4850','8','Instructional Design & Delivery for Diverse Learners','4','The purpose of this course is to provide pre-service teachers with conceptual and application knowledge of instructional design and delivery in English Language Arts and Social Studies for students with exceptionalities and all learners. Focus will also be placed on students’ areas of strengths and needs, and how these inform the choice and application of instructional methods such as scaffolds, differentiation, and Universal Design for Learning for learners. This course will also cover research','Undergraduate'),
('ED4890','8','Students with Neurodevelopmental Disabilities and Special Health Care','4','Needs Credits: 4 This course is focused on the collaborative practices that enable individuals with neurodevelopmental disabilities and other health care needs to reach their maximum potential and lead meaningful lives. It explores issues related to assessment and treatment of children with, or at risk for neurodevelopmental disabilities and their families. Students examine current and historical experiences of learners with developmental disabilities, and analyze different theoretical underpinn','Undergraduate'),
('ED5600','8','Spanish and Methods of Teaching in the Content Areas for Bilingual Teachers','4','This course intends to provide pre-service teachers with opportunities to listen, speak, read and write Spanish in the academic contexts of the different content areas. The academic language necessary to teach in each content area will be practiced within the exploration and discussion of best practices and methods to teach content in a bilingual classroom. Bilingual children’s literature will be introduced and used throughout the course. Active participation in both preparation for class and in','Undergraduate'),
('ED5890','8','Observation, Practicum, and Seminar','4','This course provides an opportunity for middle childhood and adolescence education majors to experience the school environment prior to student teaching. Candidates spend 100 hours in schools for the purpose of observation of instruction and other school activities, interaction with children and teachers, and reflection. This course is taken in conjunction with a Methods in Teaching course, and is conducted under the supervision of a university supervisor. Students participate in a weekly semina','Undergraduate'),
('ED5900','8','Student Teaching in Secondary School and Seminar (Grades 7-12)','12','This is the capstone student teaching experience for candidates seeking certification in adolescence education in biology, chemistry, mathematics, social studies and Spanish. This course provides an opportunity for candidates to apply knowledge and skills developed in coursework under the guidance of a university supervisor and cooperating teacher. This course is a full-time commitment, with half the semester spent at grades 7-9 and half at grades 10-12. Students participate in a weekly seminar.','Undergraduate'),
('ED5910','8','Student Teaching in Middle School and Seminar, Grades (5-9)','12','This is the capstone student teaching experience for candidates seeking certification in middle childhood education in biology, chemistry, mathematics, and Spanish. This course provides an opportunity for candidates to apply knowledge and skills developed in coursework under the guidance of a university supervisor and cooperating teacher. This course is a full-time commitment, with half the semester spent at grades 5-6 and half at grades 7-9. Students participate in a weekly seminar.','Undergraduate'),
('ED5925','8','Teaching Linguistically and Culturally Diverse Students','4','The course is designed to prepare early childhood and elementary teachers to acknowledge and incorporate the valuable cultural and linguistic resources of the diverse children enrolled in U.S. schools to enrich the schooling experiences of all children. The course will provide an overview of applied linguistics, theories of second language acquisition, and culturally sustaining pedagogies. Teacher candidates will employ research-based planning, instruction, and assessment strategies to meet the ','Undergraduate'),
('ED5925H2020','8','Building a Classroom Community for All Learners','4','The course is designed to prepare early childhood and elementary teachers to acknowledge and incorporate the valuable cultural and linguistic resources of the diverse children enrolled in U.S. schools to enrich the schooling experiences of all children. The course will provide an overview of applied linguistics, theories of second language acquisition, and culturally sustaining pedagogies. Teacher candidates will employ research-based planning, instruction, and assessment strategies to meet the ','Undergraduate'),
('ED5926','8','Current Topics in Bilingual Education','2','This course will provide a historical overview of bilingual education in the U.S. as well as exploration of the current policies and programs for bilingual learners. Time will be dedicated to exploring the bilingual populations as well as important sub-groups within it including newcomers, SIFE, and bilingual students enrolled in special education programs. This course will focus on ways that teachers and schools can acknowledge and incorporate the valuable cultural and linguistic resources of b','Undergraduate'),
('ED5930','8','Internship: Meeting the Needs of Diverse Learners','4','This course is the first of two fieldwork experiences required for Special Education majors in their senior year. The course provides opportunities for pre-service teachers to apply the knowledge and skills developed in coursework to a classroom setting that supports children with disabilities. Students are assigned to area schools for 100 hours. Students are also required to participate in weekly seminar sessions.','Undergraduate'),
('ED5940','8','Student Teaching and Seminar','12','Student Teaching and Senior Seminar is the final student teaching field experience required of Teacher Education majors. The course provides opportunity to refine and reflect upon the knowledge, skills, and attitudes needed to become effective elementary school teachers. Students are assigned to area schools and are required to student teach 5 days a week for 15 weeks. Students are also required to participate in seminars in certification preparation sessions.','Undergraduate'),
('ED5940B','8','Student Teaching & Seminar - Bilingual','12','Student Teaching and Senior Seminar is the final student teaching field experience required of Teacher Education majors. The course provides opportunity to refine and reflect upon the knowledge, skills, and attitudes needed to become effective elementary school teachers. Students are assigned to area schools and are required to student teach 5 days a week for 15 weeks. Students are also required to participate in seminars in certification preparation sessions.','Undergraduate'),
('ED5960','8','Student Teaching & Seminar in Diverse Classrooms: Teachers as','6','Leaders/Advocates for All Students and their Families Credits: 6 Student Teaching is the final student teaching field experience required of Teacher Education majors. The course provides an opportunity to refine and reflect upon the knowledge, skills, and attitudes needed to become effective elementary school teachers. Students are assigned to area schools and are required to student teach 5 days a week in two rotations for a total of 15 weeks within two grade levels (PreK-6 and 7-12). Students ','Undergraduate'),
('ED5960B','8','Student Teaching & Seminar - Bilingual','6','This is the first half of the final student teaching experience for students seeking dual certification as elementary and special education teachers. The course provides opportunities for students to refine and reflect upon the knowledge, skills, and attitudes needed to become effective elementary school teachers. Students are assigned to selected area schools and required to student teach 5 days a week for 15 weeks. Students are also required to participate in weekly certification preparation s','Undergraduate'),
